              "step1": "Đoạn G: For all its brilliance , the Reyburns' steam car never made money . At something like 3,200 pounds in 1921 it lay within reach only of the very rich . Nor did it help that no two examples of the Mark V were ever quite alike , since Cornelius Reyburn could never stop adjusting the design . Fewer than forty of these remarkable cars had left the works by the time the firm closed in 1928 . Reyburn himself never wavered: right up to his death in 1958 he insisted that steam was every bit the equal of petrol, and quite possibly better . The evidence rather supports him . Many of the surviving Mark Vs still run perfectly well , a few having covered more than half a million miles on nothing but routine servicing . Remarkably, an unaltered Mark V burns cleanly enough to meet the emission limits now enforced in London , rules with a reputation for being rigorous . The technology is not without its awkward problems , yet it is hard not to wonder what a steam car might achieve with modern materials and computers . With so much pressure nowadays to make vehicles perform better and pollute less , the return of the steam car is not so far-fetched an idea . Câu (1)–(4): nêu thất bại thương mại làm nền: giá cao, các xe không đồng nhất và sản lượng thấp trước khi công ty đóng cửa. Câu (5)–(8): chuyển sang giá trị kỹ thuật: bằng chứng về độ bền và khả năng đáp ứng chuẩn khí thải hiện nay ủng hộ quan điểm của Reyburn. Câu (9)–(10): thừa nhận hạn chế nhưng chốt khả năng xe hơi nước trở lại để đáp ứng nhu cầu tăng hiệu năng, giảm ô nhiễm hiện nay . Đoạn G — might achieve",
