              "step1": "Đoạn A: Scattered across Easter Island (Rapa Nui to those who live there) stand several hundred colossal stone figures, the moai . Polynesian voyagers reached this isolated speck in the Pacific and were then cut off from the wider world for centuries . Everything the moai demanded — and some rise more than nine metres and weigh over 6,000 kilos — came from the island's own resources . Yet the Spanish sailors who landed in 1740 found a people still living , technologically, in the Stone Age . Shaped with stone tools alone, the figures had somehow been carried many kilometres to the massive stone platforms on which they stood , without draught animals or wheels . Just who made them stayed unresolved deep into the twentieth century . The Danish anthropologist Aksel Vendt held that seafarers from the South American mainland were responsible , while the popular writer Lucien Berne credited survivors of a drowned mid-ocean continent . Linguistic, archaeological and genetic findings have since settled the matter — the builders were the islanders' own ancestors — though how they shifted such weights remains unproven . Island tradition insists the figures simply strode into place ; scholars have more often assumed they were dragged with ropes and wooden rollers . Câu (1)–(5): giới thiệu moai và bối cảnh khiến việc tạo, di chuyển tượng trở thành điều khó giải thích. Câu (6)–(7): đặt câu hỏi ai dựng tượng , rồi nêu hai giả thuyết cạnh tranh . Câu (8): câu hỏi về người dựng tượng đã có lời giải , nhưng cách vận chuyển vẫn chưa được chứng minh . Câu (9): tiếp tục nhánh vận chuyển bằng lời kể truyền thống và giả định của học giả. Đoạn A — Just who made them",
