              "step1": "Đoạn F: The two archaeologists are also sure the settlers were not wholly to blame for the loss of the trees . Preserved nuts from the extinct Rapa Nui palm bear tiny grooves gnawed by stowaway rats — creatures that arrived with the earliest settlers and, by Marsh and Brennan's reckoning, would have overrun the island within a few years . By eating the seeds , they would have stopped the slow-growing palms from regenerating , dooming the forest regardless of any clearing by the settlers ; the rats surely took birds' eggs too . Nor , they argue, is there evidence that island society collapsed when the palms did . They see a population that grew quickly and then held steady until Europeans arrived with diseases against which the islanders had no defence . Later, in the nineteenth century, slave raiders gutted what remained , until by 1883 just 130 people were left . Câu (1): nêu lập trường — người định cư không hoàn toàn chịu trách nhiệm về việc mất cây. Câu (2)–(3): bằng chứng về rats => chúng ngăn rừng tái sinh , bất kể việc phát quang của người định cư. Câu (4)–(6): không có bằng chứng xã hội sụp đổ cùng lúc với rừng; dân số ổn định cho tới khi bệnh từ bên ngoài xuất hiện, rồi tiếp tục bị bắt nô lệ tàn phá . Đoạn F — not wholly to blame",
