              "step1": "Đoạn D: How the Industrial Revolution had come about was something Trevisan puzzled over for years . Around the mid-eighteenth century, historians had fixed on one curious development that cried out for explanation . Britain's population had barely stirred between roughly 1650 and 1740 , and then all at once it surged . ' Within twenty years the rate at which infants died was cut by half - in the countryside and the towns alike, and among rich and poor households alike ,' says Trevisan . ' Four explanations were put forward . Had the viruses and bacteria of the day suddenly altered ? That looked improbable . Had medicine been transformed ? Hardly - this was a good hundred years before Renwick overhauled surgery . Had the surroundings people lived in shifted ? Farming had improved enough to clear malaria from some districts , but such gains were slight , and proper sanitation remained a nineteenth-century development . That leaves only diet - yet the figures for height and weight were falling , so if anything food had grown worse .' Every effort to explain the plunge in child deaths , in short , hit a wall . Câu (1)–(2): đặt vấn đề cần tìm lời giải, rồi hướng sự chú ý vào một hiện tượng cụ thể. Câu (3)–(4): dân số Britain tăng vọt , đồng thời tỷ lệ tử vong ở trẻ nhỏ giảm . Câu (5)–(12): lần lượt kiểm tra bốn cách giải thích , nhưng chúng đều khó xảy ra hoặc không giải thích thỏa đáng hiện tượng. Câu (13): chốt lại rằng các nỗ lực tìm nguyên nhân vẫn bế tắc . Đoạn D — Britain's population",
