              "step1": "Đoạn E: The timing of that population surge could hardly have suited the Industrial Revolution better , coming exactly when workers were wanted . ' Once a society starts shifting towards industry , packing people closely together makes sound economic sense ,' Trevisan notes , ' but disease comes with it , above all from human waste .' Trawling through old records brought to light evidence that water-borne illness , dysentery in particular, did alter in frequency at just that moment . Whatever the population was drinking , he concluded, must have counted for much in holding such diseases down . ' Beer was our drink ,' he says . ' For generations the hops added to preserve it - a potent destroyer of bacteria - kept the English safe . Then , late in the seventeenth century, malt, the grain from which beer is brewed, was taxed . The poorest fell back on water and on gin , and by the 1720s deaths were climbing once again . After that the rate fell away sharply - and what lay behind that ?' Câu (1)–(2): tăng dân số thuận lợi cho công nghiệp hóa, nhưng tập trung dân cư kéo theo bệnh tật. Câu (3)–(4): bệnh lây qua nước thay đổi => chuyển hướng điều tra sang đồ uống . Câu (5)–(6): giai đoạn đầu, người Britain uống bia và được hops bảo vệ. Câu (7)–(8): malt bị đánh thuế => người nghèo chuyển sang nước ; tử vong tăng trở lại. Câu (9): tử vong sau đó giảm, mở ra câu hỏi về nguyên nhân tiếp theo. Đoạn E — Whatever the population was drinking",
